{"chunk_id": "c84eb292ceb9", "text": "In the aftermath of the dice game, Bhima burns with fury while Vidura warns the Kuru assembly that their treatment of Draupadi is a grave moral failure that will destroy the dynasty. He argues that Yudhishthira could not rightfully stake Draupadi after he had already lost himself. As ominous animal cries echo through the palace, wise elders recognize the signs of impending disaster. Alarmed, Dhritarashtra rebukes Duryodhana and offers Draupadi boons to avert catastrophe. Instead of revenge, Draupadi asks first for Yudhishthira\u2019s freedom and then for the liberation of her other husbands. She refuses a third boon, citing restraint and virtue. Even Karna admits that her act saved the Pandavas, comparing her to a boat rescuing men drowning in an ocean of misery\u2014highlighting her wisdom, restraint, and moral authority in a moment of crisis.\nAfter ominous portents, Dhritarashtra grants Draupadi boons freeing the Pandavas; Karna acknowledges her saving them, and Bhima fumes at the humiliation.\nThemes: justice and dharma, omens and fate, humiliation and honor, royal authority, loyalty in marriage", "vector": [-0.020560353994369507, -0.03805362060666084, -0.013835829682648182, 0.0006760149844922125, -0.01861967332661152, 0.027793798595666885, -0.023328877985477448, 0.016570424661040306, -0.008305569179356098, -0.02088606357574463, 0.02369529940187931, 0.008766989223659039, 0.015946149826049805, 0.0191218089312315, -0.006100250408053398, 0.02294888347387314, 0.05097339302301407, 0.0032231579534709454, 0.048720575869083405, -0.024740280583500862, -0.009818756952881813, -0.011264088563621044, -0.0009279301739297807, 0.024251718074083328, -0.023261021822690964, 0.008237713016569614, 0.02289459854364395, -0.019691798835992813, 0.005017947871237993, -0.013645832426846027, -0.014860454015433788, 0.0065922061912715435, 0.009723758324980736, -0.015159020200371742, -0.028092365711927414, 0.02146962471306324, -0.0030789640732109547, -0.0